  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dense fog across the Susq Valley early today,\notherwise unseasonably mild through Friday with 2.5 weeks-long \ndry spell now over.\n\nA slow moving and weak cold front over Eastern Pennsylvania will \nbe the focus for some morning showers across the region to the \nEast of the Route 15/Interstate 81 corridors. A secondary cold \nfront will push through Northwestern PA later this morning and \nmove across the rest of Central PA and the Susquehanna Valley this\nafternoon. \n\nOne more day of unseasonably warm temperatures across much of\nthe CWA, though even highs in the mid to upper 50s across the NW\nMtns is still quite mild for early Nov.\n\nA dry slot with plenty of thin spots in the high clouds and near\ncalm air in the weak P-gradient between the aforementioned\ncfronts was leading to areas of dense fog across the Lower and\nMiddle Susq Valley early today. \n\nThe approach of the secondary front from the west will stir up a\nlight SW to Westerly breeze within a few hours after sunrise and\nthe fog should improve quickly during the second half of the\nmorning commute. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending cooler this weekend with lake enhanced \nrain showers beneath upper level low\n\nUpdate...\n00Z guidance continues to highlight the trough of the upcoming \ncooldown occurring Sat morning when a pocket of -7 to -8C 850 \nmb temps pivots through NRN PA accompanied by deep moisture and \nsome mesoscale focus for lift and occasional rain/wet snow \nshowers along and in the wake of yet a 3rd cfrontal boundary \nwith a boost of enhanced orographic lift. \n\nIts not out of the question to see a light slushy coating of\nsnow during the mid/late morning hours Sat at elevations above\n2100 ft MSL across the NW and over 2600 MSL in the Laurel\nHighlands - the first of this mild/very pleasant fall season so\nfar. GFS is by far the coldest of the model guidance but it \nshould be highlighted than Lake Erie is still 60-62F (near or\nslightly over record warm water temps for this date). This will\nundoubtedly temper this cooler guidance. \n\nPrevious disc... \n\nWe have been constantly been pulling NBM (mean) temp guidance \ndown closer to MOS blend for Sat, and we've now done it more so \nwith continued support from many sources. We have also pulled \ntemps up a few degs for Sat night due to the same clouds and \nalso wind which should keep us more- mixed. \n\nSeasonably cooler/breezy pattern to start the first full \nweekend of November. The passage of closed H5 low followed by \nmean cyclonic flow favors persistent clouds and periods of lake \nenhanced rain over the western and central Alleghenies on \nSaturday. We were keen to manually adjust max temps a few \ndegrees lower on 11/7 to account for the cooler pattern shift \nwhich looks to continue into early next week. If the current \nforecast holds, it will be the first below average daytime high \nin three weeks!",
